######
## This script analyze the log generated by ausearch (reading by default /var/log/audit/audit.log)
## It returns a text containing :
##     - All the SELinux rules that are needed (ie what audit2allow do normally)
##     - The commands that caused each rules and some useful info about them
##     - A PID Tree section showing process hierarchy from EXECVE syscalls (with PID, command, SELinux context)
## The output can be given to my 'se_policy_merger' tool to pack it into a policy.
## As of now, this script only understand "allow_rules", which mean a few AVC/USER_AVC error won't be resolved with it (for example an invalid context or constraint violation)
##   Don't forget to use audit2allow which is a more advanced analyzer if this happen.
## It doesn't take in account SELINUX_ERR nor USER_SELINUX_ERR log messages
##
## It is possible to give in input one (or more) --files that have been created with this tool to merge them together and/or with the logs analysed in live.
## Usage: --files file1 file2 ... (each file as a separate argument)
##
## Log Memory: The script remembers which audit log entries have been analyzed (using --state-file) to avoid reprocessing.
######

######
## Usage example :
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --log ./logs > rules_with_explication.txt
##
## With state file for incremental analysis (remembers what was already analyzed):
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --log ./logs --state-file ./analyzer_state.json > rules.txt
##
## Without PID tree:
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --log ./logs --no-tree > rules.txt
##
## JSON output (structured, for programmatic use):
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --log ./logs --json-dest ./output.json
##
## JSON round-trip (load JSON, merge with logs, output JSON):
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --json-files ./prev.json --json-dest ./merged.json
##
## Mixed: human-readable files + JSON files as input, both outputs:
## se_log_analyser --key $hostname --files rules.txt --json-files prev.json --dest merged.txt --json-dest merged.json
######


